    starterCode: "def beam_search_step(current_beams: list[dict], candidate_log_probs: list[list[float]], beam_width: int, eos_token_id: int) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Expand active beams by candidate log probabilities and prune to top-B.\n    \n    Args:\n        current_beams: List of dicts with 'sequence', 'score', and 'is_done'.\n        candidate_log_probs: Vocabulary log probabilities for each beam.\n        beam_width: Number of active hypotheses to retain (B).\n        eos_token_id: End of sequence token ID.\n    Returns:\n        dict with 'beams' (top beam_width dicts) and 'all_done' (bool).\n    \"\"\"\n    # Your implementation here\n    pass\n",
    mission: "Explore multiple high-probability generation hypotheses simultaneously using Beam Search.",
    taskDescription: "Implement `beam_search_step(current_beams, candidate_log_probs, beam_width, eos_token_id)`. For each beam in `current_beams`: if `is_done` is True, carry it forward unchanged; otherwise expand it across all vocabulary tokens $v$, updating `score = round(beam['score'] + log_prob[v], 4)` and setting `is_done = True` if $v == \text{eos\_token\_id}$. Sort all candidate hypotheses descending by score (breaking ties lexicographically by sequence). Prune to `beam_width` entries. Return dict with `beams` and `all_done` (True if every pruned beam is done). Raise `ValueError` if `beam_width <= 0` or if input list lengths mismatch.",
    libraryPolicyText: "Libraries allowed · Pure Python earns +10 bonus XP",
    bonusPoints: 10,
